## Real-world testing scenarios

### Scenario 1: Detecting when iteration isn't working

```
Tracking iteration cycles on Guidance v1.0:

Week 1: First-pass acceptance 40%
Week 2: (Updated guidance) 52%
Week 3: (Added examples) 61%
Week 4: (Refined constraints) 63%
Week 5: (Smaller tweaks) 63%
Week 6: (More tweaks) 64%

Signal: Diminishing returns after Week 4. Small tweaks stopped moving needle.

Decision: FREEZE guidance. Run experiment on completely different intervention:
- Option A: Train editors (maybe humans are the bottleneck)
- Option B: Test different AI model (maybe base model matters)
- Option C: Oppositional review (catch biased guidance)

Choose: Option C (oppositional review), test if we can catch edge cases our guidance misses.
```

### Scenario 2: Detecting bias in guidance

```
Observation: "Disaster Recovery" content fails 22% of time; "Urban Planning" fails 6%.

Hypothesis: 
IF guidance is trained primarily on Urban Planning examples,
THEN it's over-fit and will fail on other content types

Test:
1. Extract only Urban Planning examples from EXAMPLE_CONTENTS.md
2. Check if guidance mentions "Disaster" concepts or only urban-focused vocabulary
3. Re-train guidance with balanced mix (50% Urban, 50% Disaster)
4. Test on held-out Disaster Recovery content

Result:
- Failure rate on Disaster content: 22% → 9%
- Decision: Balance exemplar corpus; update curation criteria
```

### Scenario 3: Testing whether examples help

```
Hypothesis:
IF we show AI exemplars of good vs. bad titles (with annotations),
THEN title accuracy improves from 70% to 85%

Test:
Condition A: Guidance text only ("Write titles 50–80 characters")
Condition B: Guidance text + 5 exemplars (showing good titles with annotations)
Measure: % of AI-generated titles that pass editor review

Result: 70% vs. 85% acceptance (p = 0.03)
Decision: ADOPT exemplars; add to all guidance documents
```

